SpadeIDE for Debian / Ubuntu
============================

Which install?
--------------

  Only you (default)     .deb → SpadeIDE Agent from the app grid
                         No Unix password on the iPad; files under your $HOME.

  Shared desktop /       Enable the system unit (below). Each iPad signs in
  multi-user             with a Unix user once, then sees only that home.

Desktop (.deb) — one person
---------------------------

After install, open **SpadeIDE Agent** from the app grid. It sits in the
system tray with a pairing QR — same idea as the Mac menu-bar app.

  spadeide-gui
  spadeide desktop

Default: runs as **your** Unix account (user unit). Explorer/editor stay
under your `$HOME`. No Unix login sheet.

Shared desktop / multi-user (privileged broker)
-----------------------------------------------

Same code path as a server. The packaged system unit runs as root; each
iPad Unix-logs in once and is jailed to that account's home.

  systemctl --user disable --now spade-agent   # stop the per-user copy
  sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/spade-agent && sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/spade-agent
  sudo systemctl enable --now spade-agent
  sudo spadeide pair --no-start

Do not run `sudo spadeide` to start a second listener — use the unit above.

On the iPad: Pair → Unix Login (username + password, once) → Open Project.
Password change, lock, or deleted user forces login again. Unix root refused.
Terminal is that user’s normal login shell (same groups as SSH).

Day to day
----------

  spadeide              start if needed, show QR + pair URL (user install)
  sudo spadeide pair --no-start
                        QR only when the system unit is already running
  spadeide status       paired / online tablets
  spadeide stop
  spadeide reset        forget paired devices

Several tablets can stay paired. Each bare `spadeide` run issues a fresh
pair secret for a new iPad.

  sudo apt install ./spadeide-agent_*.deb

LAN discovery (IP changes): sudo apt install avahi-daemon avahi-utils

Unix login needs `unix_chkpwd` from libpam-modules (installed by default on
Ubuntu Desktop and Server). Full docs: site → Docs → Linux agent.
